+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a service or weighs an option no decision record settles. These tenets say which trade-offs
+larder favors. Written after [the pantry search discussion](../notes/issue-70.md) raised the cost of operating one
+more service again and asked that future proposals start from the rule already used for sessions and jobs.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Prefer what we already run and can restore
+
+Larder's starting point is one database and one web app. Before proposing another service for volunteers to
+operate, show both that it does something Postgres cannot and that two volunteers can restore it. This rules out
+adding a service just because it is a familiar solution, or relying on its proposer as the only person who can
+recover it. A proposal starts with the unmet need, what was tried in Postgres, and who can restore the new service.
+
+The rule grew out of operating experience. In 2023 Redis for sessions was turned down because only one volunteer
+would have run it; [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) records the choice and
+its alternatives. Redis was later used for Celery, and its queue failure exposed the cost of depending on one
+volunteer for recovery; [run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) records that
+incident and the replacement. Both needs could be met by Postgres.
+
+The pantry search proposal applied the same rule: search for "rice" took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown".
+The proposer reported that Postgres full-text search on the item name took 40 ms and found it, and chose that
+instead of Elasticsearch. Those results are evidence from the discussion, not a search implementation in this
+repository.
+
+External services remain an option: the card gateway is already outside larder, and a hosted service that nobody
+on the volunteer team has to run is acceptable. The rule limits the operational burden volunteers take on; it does
+not prohibit every external dependency.
